If there is such a quality as 'brightness', it should indeed stop gaze 
attacks (i.e. you're too bright to look at). Perhaps it could be a new 
intrinsic conferred by White Dragons and their scales.

It is debatable whether monster blindness should prevent gaze attacks; 
certainly something like a blindfold would, but what if they've been 
blinded by a camera flash? Their eyes are still open, and looking into 
them should still affect you... then again, if they can't see your eyes 
to meet your gaze directly, will the gaze attack work?

> 
> It's just occurred to me that a pyrolisk's gaze attack might actually
> fall into the latter category, so perhaps only monster blindness should
> protect against it?

It would make sense to have two types of gaze attacks, one that just 
requires the monster to look at you, and one where you must meet its 
gaze. The first is, as you describe, only affected by monster blindness. 
The second would be negated by either of you being blind.

Also, the first one should be reflected by reflection and mirrors, and 
the second one only by mirrors.

